# Line endings. This file is the single most important thing in the repository
# for anyone on Windows.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf=true` is the default on Windows installs, and it rewrites
# LF to CRLF on checkout. Do that to a bash script and every line ends in a
# carriage return that bash treats as part of the command:
#
#     $'\r': command not found
#     syntax error near unexpected token `$'{\r''
#
# The script is not broken. It is byte-for-byte what the author wrote, plus one
# invisible character per line, and nobody reading it can see the problem. The
# failure lands on a Windows user's first `leo` invocation and looks like leo
# being broken rather than like a checkout setting.
#
# So: every file leo executes or sources is marked LF, permanently, regardless
# of what the developer's git is configured to do.

# JavaScript — this is leo. Node tolerates a carriage return where bash did
# not, so a CRLF checkout no longer breaks the program outright; it is still
# pinned, because a file that arrives different from how it was written makes
# every diff noisy in exactly the place nobody needs more of it.
*.js            text eol=lf
leo             text eol=lf

# Shell — the test suite, still executed by bash.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Everything leo writes into a repository. These are read by agents and spliced
# by awk; a stray CR ends up inside a generated AGENTS.md block, inside a task
# file's to-do, and inside commit messages.
*.md            text eol=lf
templates/**    text eol=lf
.leo/**         text eol=lf

# Config that is sourced as shell. A CR here becomes part of the value:
# TEST_CMD="npm test"^M runs a command that does not exist.
.leo/config     text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
LICENSE         text eol=lf
VERSION         text eol=lf
package.json    text eol=lf

# Never touched.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.gz            binary
*.tgz           binary
